#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186625973 :question/problem "The inequality $4 \\le t < 14$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0}\\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nFor the given inequality\n\n$$\n4 \\le t < 14\n$$\n\nwe note the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $4$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $[$.\n- The right endpoint $14$ *is not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $)$.\n\nTherefore, the interval that corresponds to the given inequality is\n\n$$\nt ∈ [4, 14)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186625997 :question/problem "The vector $\\overrightarrow{ST}$ has magnitude $14$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{ST}$?" :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{ST}$, we divide $\\overrightarrow{ST}$ by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{\\overrightarrow{ST}}{|\\,\\overrightarrow{ST}\\,|}=\\frac{1}{14}\\overrightarrow{ST}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186625999 :question/problem "The vector $c$ has magnitude $25$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $c$." :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $c$, we divide $c$ by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{c}{|\\,c\\,|}=\\frac{1}{25}c\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186626002 :question/problem "The vector $\\overrightarrow{CD}$ has magnitude $21$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{CD}$?" :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{CD}$, we divide $\\overrightarrow{CD}$ by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{\\overrightarrow{CD}}{|\\,\\overrightarrow{CD}\\,|}=\\frac{1}{21}\\overrightarrow{CD}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186626012 :question/problem "The vector $\\overrightarrow{ST}$ has magnitude $15$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{ST}$?" :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{ST}$, we divide $\\overrightarrow{ST}$ by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{\\overrightarrow{ST}}{|\\,\\overrightarrow{ST}\\,|}=\\frac{1}{15}\\overrightarrow{ST}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186626013 :question/problem "The vector $a$ has magnitude $9$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $a$." :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $a$, we divide $a$ by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{a}{|\\,a\\,|}=\\frac{1}{9}a\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186626871 :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda (-9a - 3b)$ and $-3a - b$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "Since the vectors are equal, we can write\n\n$$\n-3a - b=\\lambda (-9a - 3b).\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the left-hand side, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}(-9a - 3b)=\\lambda (-9a - 3b).\n$$\n\nNotice that we have $(-9a - 3b)$ on both sides. So, let's define a new vector $u$ as\n\n$$\nu=-9a - 3b.\n$$\n\nThen, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}\\,u=\\lambda u.\n$$\n\nSince $u$ is non-zero,\n\n$$\n\\lambda=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186626886 :question/problem "Given that $-3a - 3b$ and $\\lambda (-4a - 4b)$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "Since the vectors are equal, we can write\n\n$$\n-3a - 3b=\\lambda (-4a - 4b).\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the left-hand side, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}(-4a - 4b)=\\lambda (-4a - 4b).\n$$\n\nNotice that we have $(-4a - 4b)$ on both sides. So, let's define a new vector $u$ as\n\n$$\nu=-4a - 4b.\n$$\n\nThen, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}\\,u=\\lambda u.\n$$\n\nSince $u$ is non-zero,\n\n$$\n\\lambda=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186626887 :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda (4a + 7b)$ and $-8a - 14b$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "Since the vectors are equal, we can write\n\n$$\n-8a - 14b=\\lambda (4a + 7b).\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the left-hand side, which gives\n\n$$\n-2(4a + 7b)=\\lambda (4a + 7b).\n$$\n\nNotice that we have $(4a + 7b)$ on both sides. So, let's define a new vector $u$ as\n\n$$\nu=4a + 7b.\n$$\n\nThen, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n-2\\,u=\\lambda u.\n$$\n\nSince $u$ is non-zero,\n\n$$\n\\lambda=-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186626897 :question/problem "Given that $16a + 20b$ and $\\lambda (4a + 5b)$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "Since the vectors are equal, we can write\n\n$$\n16a + 20b=\\lambda (4a + 5b).\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the left-hand side, which gives\n\n$$\n4(4a + 5b)=\\lambda (4a + 5b).\n$$\n\nNotice that we have $(4a + 5b)$ on both sides. So, let's define a new vector $u$ as\n\n$$\nu=4a + 5b.\n$$\n\nThen, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n4\\,u=\\lambda u.\n$$\n\nSince $u$ is non-zero,\n\n$$\n\\lambda=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186626898 :question/problem "Given that $12a + 16b$ and $\\lambda (3a + 4b)$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "Since the vectors are equal, we can write\n\n$$\n12a + 16b=\\lambda (3a + 4b).\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the left-hand side, which gives\n\n$$\n4(3a + 4b)=\\lambda (3a + 4b).\n$$\n\nNotice that we have $(3a + 4b)$ on both sides. So, let's define a new vector $u$ as\n\n$$\nu=3a + 4b.\n$$\n\nThen, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n4\\,u=\\lambda u.\n$$\n\nSince $u$ is non-zero,\n\n$$\n\\lambda=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186626945 :question/problem "The vector $a$ is directed horizontally to the left and has magnitude $\\sqrt{3}$, while the vector $b$ is directed vertically up and has magnitude $\\sqrt{6}.$ Find a unit vector $u$ that is parallel to $a+b$ and has the same direction as $a+b.$\n\nType $a$ and $b$ to represent the vectors $a$ and $b,$ respectively.\n\n$u=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $a+b.$ According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $a+b$ goes from the tail of $a$ to the head of $b:$\n\n![](images/97/97b9233250b17231f8a032f1c4a15c6ff91b861a503c7a1eb50f54aa4dba9af3.png)\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $a+b,$ we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\n$$\n\nThe given directions are perpendicular, so we obtain a right triangle. We can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a+b|^2&=|a|^2+|b|^2\\\\\n&=(\\sqrt{3})^2+(\\sqrt{6})^2\\\\\n&=3+6\\\\\n&=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{9}=3\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u&=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{3}(a+b).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186626958 :question/problem "The vector $a$ is directed horizontally to the left and has magnitude $3 \\sqrt{3}$, while the vector $b$ is directed vertically up and has magnitude $2 \\sqrt{7}.$ Find a unit vector $u$ that is parallel to $a+b$ and has the same direction as $a+b.$\n\nType $a$ and $b$ to represent the vectors $a$ and $b,$ respectively.\n\n$u=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $a+b.$ According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $a+b$ goes from the tail of $a$ to the head of $b:$\n\n![](images/ae/ae50887736a1299ced9ed6dfa13c31b4494eb24a68fffc482eca525ea71bc162.png)\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $a+b,$ we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\n$$\n\nThe given directions are perpendicular, so we obtain a right triangle. We can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a+b|^2&=|a|^2+|b|^2\\\\\n&=(3 \\sqrt{3})^2+(2 \\sqrt{7})^2\\\\\n&=27+28\\\\\n&=55\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{55}=\\sqrt{55}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u&=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{55}}(a+b).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186626962 :question/problem "The vector $a$ is directed horizontally to the right and has magnitude $18$, while the vector $b$ is directed vertically up and has magnitude $2.$ Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $a+b.$ According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $a+b$ goes from the tail of $a$ to the head of $b:$\n\n![](images/a3/a3f2345bc172424550ae61158e8ae1e8a9d8872b0a1e877c6eae66fa6f6a4b60.png)\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $a+b,$ we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\n$$\n\nThe given directions are perpendicular, so we obtain a right triangle. We can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a+b|^2&=|a|^2+|b|^2\\\\\n&=(18)^2+(2)^2\\\\\n&=324+4\\\\\n&=328\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{328}=2 \\sqrt{82}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u&=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{82}}(a+b).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186626963 :question/problem "The vector $a$ is directed horizontally to the left and has magnitude $\\sqrt{17}$, while the vector $b$ is directed vertically up and has magnitude $\\sqrt{6}.$ Find a unit vector $u$ that is parallel to $a+b$ and has the same direction as $a+b.$\n\nType $a$ and $b$ to represent the vectors $a$ and $b,$ respectively.\n\n$u=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $a+b.$ According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $a+b$ goes from the tail of $a$ to the head of $b:$\n\n![](images/de/def8d44d6e773c9385c62559fccd83ea638924222fcce09b05806bf99beb71ac.png)\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $a+b,$ we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\n$$\n\nThe given directions are perpendicular, so we obtain a right triangle. We can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a+b|^2&=|a|^2+|b|^2\\\\\n&=(\\sqrt{17})^2+(\\sqrt{6})^2\\\\\n&=17+6\\\\\n&=23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{23}=\\sqrt{23}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u&=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{23}}(a+b).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186626965 :question/problem "The vector $a$ is directed horizontally to the right and has magnitude $13$, while the vector $b$ is directed vertically up and has magnitude $29.$ Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $a+b.$ According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $a+b$ goes from the tail of $a$ to the head of $b:$\n\n![](images/98/98b4899d5f2729612b63f6e37d24510a07ff7fe899f4b437f60278d7805c1029.png)\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $a+b,$ we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\n$$\n\nThe given directions are perpendicular, so we obtain a right triangle. We can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a+b|^2&=|a|^2+|b|^2\\\\\n&=(13)^2+(29)^2\\\\\n&=169+841\\\\\n&=1010\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{1010}=\\sqrt{1010}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u&=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1010}}(a+b).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186627942 :question/problem "![](images/44/4435e904dc532907bc85ed8ac6ecd52b0320736e1ef2fa3e2746a40472577829.png)\n\nThe vector $a$ is depicted above. Which of the following diagrams shows the vectors $-\\frac{1}{2}a$ and $2a$?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that both $-\\frac{1}{2}a$ and $2a$ must be parallel to $a$.\n\nNow, for the vector $-\\frac{1}{2}a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $-\\frac{1}{2}a$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $-\\frac{1}{2}a$ have opposite directions since the factor $-\\frac{1}{2}$ is negative.\n\n![](images/4e/4e1721331e0fa7e4240b1d09426e57fdc8e48052bd72dd9733cbcc006fddfb38.png)\n\nNext, for the vector $2a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $2a$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $2a$ have the same direction since the factor $2$ is positive.\n\nThe required vectors are shown below.\n\n![](images/4c/4c184c372b1dd3618f46ccb5a4ec7cb131cc6de29dc0641adc9cb54a516d7d3d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186627945 :question/problem "![](images/1f/1f94ae6ec9c86b20ce4d872836d19739244668fdd6347d987046fd79c354fb1f.png)\n\nThe vector $a$ is depicted above. Which of the following diagrams shows the vectors $\\frac{3}{2}a$ and $-2a$?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that both $\\frac{3}{2}a$ and $-2a$ must be parallel to $a$.\n\nNow, for the vector $\\frac{3}{2}a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $\\frac{3}{2}a$ is $\\frac{3}{2}$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $\\frac{3}{2}a$ have the same direction since the factor $\\frac{3}{2}$ is positive.\n\n![](images/c6/c6022d0b5774dbb5dba7427fa75774fc9294301933c7008d91f01d23ca40b040.png)\n\nNext, for the vector $-2a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $-2a$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $-2a$ have opposite directions since the factor $-2$ is negative.\n\nThe required vectors are shown below.\n\n![](images/d4/d4a0b82b7cc4135940464404e7429a3949d10577a43a0bffebd392c257485346.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186627956 :question/problem "![](images/49/497768be6b0c83d24209881af4ce3038e341de6b81d0e9436405c4153bf45360.png)\n\nThe vector $a$ is depicted above. Which of the following diagrams shows the vectors $-2a$ and $\\frac{3}{2}a$?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that both $-2a$ and $\\frac{3}{2}a$ must be parallel to $a$.\n\nNow, for the vector $-2a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $-2a$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $-2a$ have opposite directions since the factor $-2$ is negative.\n\n![](images/ae/ae36e5e29400fc7172ca1173a49d3a6087c346db954ea3c88f8d85fa6689762f.png)\n\nNext, for the vector $\\frac{3}{2}a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $\\frac{3}{2}a$ is $\\frac{3}{2}$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $\\frac{3}{2}a$ have the same direction since the factor $\\frac{3}{2}$ is positive.\n\nThe required vectors are shown below.\n\n![](images/f6/f65cd32c91eca34ba70bd1207dc7751091ea52fccb4708b68b462419bf74a84c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186627957 :question/problem "![](images/cf/cf412b0c96a9086796973f4a92f855b9a2722ae739dd89ff0665e3ee43fe086e.png)\n\nThe vector $a$ is depicted above. Which of the following diagrams shows the vectors $-3a$ and $\\frac{3}{2}a$?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that both $-3a$ and $\\frac{3}{2}a$ must be parallel to $a$.\n\nNow, for the vector $-3a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $-3a$ is $3$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $-3a$ have opposite directions since the factor $-3$ is negative.\n\n![](images/fe/fec41e0bb69863877ff80245f444f39d93d78c1c3f49ae1dcfdadfa3c33f2a6b.png)\n\nNext, for the vector $\\frac{3}{2}a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $\\frac{3}{2}a$ is $\\frac{3}{2}$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $\\frac{3}{2}a$ have the same direction since the factor $\\frac{3}{2}$ is positive.\n\nThe required vectors are shown below.\n\n![](images/9c/9c53ecd9372f2c1cc8c1420d5de94e8434076582754841c7189945e29c59114b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186627967 :question/problem "![](images/93/935ba18cdd1ffec64c65eb870ca706a835cb10c4603604fc2c519bb5a7291398.png)\n\nThe vector $a$ is depicted above. Which of the following diagrams shows the vectors $-\\frac{3}{2}a$ and $3a$?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that both $-\\frac{3}{2}a$ and $3a$ must be parallel to $a$.\n\nNow, for the vector $-\\frac{3}{2}a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $-\\frac{3}{2}a$ is $\\frac{3}{2}$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $-\\frac{3}{2}a$ have opposite directions since the factor $-\\frac{3}{2}$ is negative.\n\n![](images/cd/cd326b5ee93e0fb47c3aee792a3aee05756f8582420ccfca1da1ab588701185d.png)\n\nNext, for the vector $3a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $3a$ is $3$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $3a$ have the same direction since the factor $3$ is positive.\n\nThe required vectors are shown below.\n\n![](images/b7/b7a8e357dbe7e3af5d859158eb5c785c401b26d360c4347b3ce16afdaf33230e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186628485 :question/problem "![](images/22/2262a81966190b89ebf03015e85f1c7e749efc2c4d9f8ed822309508d5b75813.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2t,\\,y=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2t)=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1))}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{8-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the double-angle formula for cosine, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .$ Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{t}{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{t}{2}) & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right) \\\\ 1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right) \\\\ 8-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =16{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{8-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 4\\cdot {\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-8(0-1) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186628577 :question/problem "![](images/cc/cce654debb192f5f2ef24c33a7fc32d545eab47a5e4b718a2ea82e314cf812c8.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $s=3t$\n\nII. $r=2p$\n\nIII. $q=-r$" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is false. The vectors $s$ and $t$ are parallel and have the same direction. The magnitude of $s$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $t$. So, $s\\ne 3t$.\n\n- Statement II is true. The vectors $r$ and $p$ are parallel and have the same direction. The magnitude of $r$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $p$. So, $r=2p$.\n\n- Statement III is false. The vectors $q$ and $r$ are parallel and have opposite directions. The magnitude of $q$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $r$. So, $q\\ne -r$.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186628583 :question/problem "![](images/7b/7b1496185812c5663cf8e07b02a8e51f9c3b07393d4c08d988cbf1fcd3179a06.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is true. The vectors $d$ and $e$ are parallel and have opposite directions. The magnitude of $d$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $e$. So, $d=-2e$.\n\n- Statement II is false. The vectors $a$ and $b$ are parallel and have the same direction. The magnitude of $a$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $b$. So, $a\\ne -b$.\n\n- Statement III is false. The vectors $d$ and $e$ are parallel and have opposite directions. The magnitude of $d$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $e$. So, $d\\ne 2e$.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186628589 :question/problem "![](images/b6/b6c26d5365880d39ef1e5170dbfad0632ca60735727a6779b20059f7b18e8687.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is false. The vectors $d$ and $b$ are parallel and have opposite directions. The magnitude of $d$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $b$. So, $d\\ne -b$.\n\n- Statement II is true. The vectors $b$ and $d$ are parallel and have opposite directions. The magnitude of $b$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$ times the magnitude of $d$. So, $b=-\\frac{1}{2}d$.\n\n- Statement III is false. The vectors $a$ and $e$ are parallel and have the same direction. The magnitude of $a$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $e$. So, $a\\ne e$.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186628594 :question/problem "![](images/7a/7ad3dd07f782811a42c9e0b06299a95c4d0ce984bdbdabd5f952ef0f6820155f.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $a=-z$\n\nII. $m=k$\n\nIII. $z=-\\frac{1}{2}a$" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is false. The vectors $a$ and $z$ are parallel and have the same direction. The magnitude of $a$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $z$. So, $a\\ne -z$.\n\n- Statement II is true. The vectors $m$ and $k$ are parallel and have the same direction. The magnitude of $m$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $k$. So, $m=k$.\n\n- Statement III is false. The vectors $z$ and $a$ are parallel and have the same direction. The magnitude of $z$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $a$. So, $z\\ne -\\frac{1}{2}a$.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186628603 :question/problem "![](images/b4/b4a29b1ca13d5ddcb342f247b8b477058586bdad0bb4a6af588d66ef15f9df3c.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $r=3p$\n\nII. $q=2t$\n\nIII. $s=-q$" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is false. The vectors $r$ and $p$ are parallel and have the same direction. The magnitude of $r$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $p$. So, $r\\ne 3p$.\n\n- Statement II is false. The vectors $q$ and $t$ are parallel and have opposite directions. The magnitude of $q$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $t$. So, $q\\ne 2t$.\n\n- Statement III is true. The vectors $s$ and $q$ are parallel and have opposite directions. The magnitude of $s$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $q$. So, $s=-q$.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186630234 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,DFG$, if $D(1,0,0)$, $F(1,2,1)$, $G(1,3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{DF}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DG}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DF} & =f-d \\\\  & =(i+2j+k)-i \\\\  & =2j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,2,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{DG} & =g-d \\\\  & =(i+3j+k)-i \\\\  & =3j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,3,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DF}\\times \\overset{\\to }{DG} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 1)i-(0\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 1)j+(0\\cdot 3-0\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =-i \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{DF}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DG}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{DF}\\times \\overset{\\to }{DG}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,DFG$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186630925 :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(6,\\theta )$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(3,\\theta -\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(3\\sqrt{2},-3\\sqrt{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{-3\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the fourth quadrant, we have $\\theta =2\\pi +{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$ Consequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(3,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=(3,\\pi ).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-3,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186631884 :question/problem "Calculate $det(M)$, if\n $$\n\\displaystyle M=\\begin{bmatrix}2x & \\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & -x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2x & \\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & -x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2x\\cdot (-x)-\\sqrt{y}\\cdot \\sqrt{y} \\\\  & =-2{x}^{2}-y\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186632064 :question/problem "Calculate $F(x)$ given that $f(x)={x}^{7}$ and ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply the power rule with $n=7$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7+1}}{7+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{8}}{8}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{8}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{8}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{8}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{8})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 8\\,{x}^{7} \\\\ &= {x}^{7}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186632066 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {x}^{12}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the power rule with $n=12$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{12}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{12+1}}{12+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{13}}{13}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{13}}{13}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{13}}{13}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{13}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{13})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{13}\\cdot 13{x}^{12} \\\\ &= {x}^{12}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186632067 :question/problem "Calculate $F(x)$ given that $f(x)={x}^{5}$ and ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply the power rule with $n=5$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5+1}}{5+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{6}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{6})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 6\\,{x}^{5} \\\\ &= {x}^{5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186632088 :question/problem "Calculate $F(x)$ given that $f(x)={x}^{19}$ and ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply the power rule with $n=19$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{19}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{19+1}}{19+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{20}}{20}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{20}}{20}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{20}}{20}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{20}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{20})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{20}\\cdot 20\\,{x}^{19} \\\\ &= {x}^{19}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186632091 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the power rule with $n=4$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{4+1}}{4+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 5{x}^{4} \\\\ &= {x}^{4}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186632107 :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of ${x}^{3/4}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$." :question/worked-solution "We can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{3}{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3/4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{3/4+1}}{(\\frac{3}{4}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/4}}{\\left(\\frac{7}{4}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{7/4}}{7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4{x}^{7/4}}{7}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{4{x}^{7/4}}{7}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{4}{7}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7/4})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{4}{7}\\cdot \\frac{7}{4}{x}^{7/4-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{3/4}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186632110 :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of ${x}^{1/3}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$." :question/worked-solution "We can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/3+1}}{(\\frac{1}{3}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4/3}}{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4/3})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}{x}^{4/3-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186632112 :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of ${x}^{5/4}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$.\n\nThe antiderivative is {{field-1}} $+C$. Enter only the term before $+C$, with no additive constant." :question/worked-solution "We can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{5}{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{5/4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{5/4+1}}{(\\frac{5}{4}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{9/4}}{\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{9/4}}{9}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4{x}^{9/4}}{9}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{4{x}^{9/4}}{9}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{4}{9}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{9/4})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{4}{9}\\cdot \\frac{9}{4}{x}^{9/4-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{5/4}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186632114 :question/problem "The antiderivative of $\\sqrt[3]{x}$ equals {{field-1}} $+C$.\n\nWork on $x>0$. Enter only the term before $+C$, with no additive constant." :question/worked-solution "Since $\\sqrt[3]{x}={x}^{1/3}$, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/3+1}}{(\\frac{1}{3}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4/3}}{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check our answer by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4/3})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}{x}^{4/3-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186632119 :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of ${x}^{1/4}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$." :question/worked-solution "We can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/4+1}}{(\\frac{1}{4}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/4}}{\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}}{5}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}}{5}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{4}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5/4})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}{x}^{5/4-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/4}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
